WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Docker Compose.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.659
Clone a repo, run one command, and get a working stack.

3
00:00:05.659 --> 00:00:08.867
That is the experience good projects are judged by.

4
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:14.303
Docker Compose declares your whole dev stack in one YAML file.

5
00:00:14.303 --> 00:00:16.900
The API, Postgres and Redis, together.

6
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.300
The standard is simple: git clone, then docker compose up, and you have a working stack.

7
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.710
It is a dev tool.

8
00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:29.067
In production, Kubernetes takes over, and Compose is its single-machine rehearsal.

9
00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:33.800
Here is the quiet superpower: networking by service name.

10
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:40.300
Your API runs alongside its dependencies, all on one private network with its own DNS.

11
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.367
It reaches the database at postgres, port fifty four thirty two.

12
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:47.817
And the cache at redis, port sixty three seventy nine.

13
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:50.033
The names come straight from the YAML.

14
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:52.933
One more detail on startup.

15
00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:56.033
Depends on controls the order services start in.

16
00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:01.033
Add healthchecks, and Compose waits until a service is ready, not merely started.

17
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:03.700
Here it is, trimmed down.

18
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.433
The API builds from the current folder and exposes port eight thousand.

19
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:10.576
Look at the database URL.

20
00:01:10.576 --> 00:01:13.968
The host is simply postgres, the service name.

21
00:01:13.968 --> 00:01:15.000
No IP address.

22
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.357
Depends on controls startup order.

23
00:01:17.357 --> 00:01:20.867
Add healthchecks and it waits for ready, not just started.

24
00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:23.437
Now a real scenario.

25
00:01:23.437 --> 00:01:25.767
A collaborator joins your project.

26
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:31.400
In the old world, that was half a day installing Postgres and fixing extension versions.

27
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.733
In the new world, they clone and run docker compose up.

28
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:39.028
Four minutes later they have the whole stack, identical to yours.

29
00:01:39.028 --> 00:01:42.100
One YAML file deletes every works for me argument.

30
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.083
Let's recap.

31
00:01:44.083 --> 00:01:46.300
One YAML declares the whole stack.

32
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.700
Services reach each other by name.

33
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.533
Depends on orders startup.

34
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:53.060
And Compose is for development.

35
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.000
Next up: Kubernetes basics.
